Thanks to both Nikola Milutinovic and Tom Webster for their swift and hel=
pfull=20
replies!! :-)

The basic consensis is that as a C compiller GCC is far to relaxed in typ=
e=20
checking hence a program that will compile with out error or warning on G=
CC=20
will most likely  bomb out on Compaq CC. =20

To get simmilar behaviour to GCC with the standard Compaq CC compiler the=
=20
following flags will have to be given to the C compiler

For 4.0x and 5.1x systems the flag would be -std

On the other hand Tom pointed me to the Developers Tool Kit version of th=
e=20
Compaq C compiler.  Which has GCC  mode compatibility switched on by defa=
ult.=20
To get GNU G++ compatibility with the Compaq C++ compiler you will have t=
o=20
issue the following flag to the compiler.=20

"-std gnu"

However the is one catch if you want to use the DTK version of the Compaq=
 C=20
compiler in that it requires the OSF-DEV licence as Tom points out which =
you=20
should this licence installed if you are compiling anything other than th=
e=20
kernel.


Also if you are compiling a lot of open source software it is recomended =
that=20
you install GNU make which is avalible from http://www.gnu.org

To download the DTK version of the Compaq CC compiler you can go to=20
http://h30097.www3.hp.com/tools.html#application

Also worth noting that they three standards of C at the moment with two f=
ormal=20
standards ( ANSI and K&R) and one informal ( GNU C) which is widely sprea=
d. =20
As Nickola replies to my wish list "An even better wish-list would be to=20
attempt standardising GNU C syntax. If it is coherent, if it brings furth=
er=20
abilities, then let's push it as a formal standard. This way we have 3 fo=
rmal=20
standards and one informal, but widely spread." Which I tend to agree wit=
h.

-greg

