Managers,

Many thanks to Alan Rollow and Chris Bryant for their responses.

Alan,

many gratefulness for its deep analysis.


########################################################################=
####
#######################

	The message listing included indicates multiple problems
	over about three weeks.  They could be unrelated.  Some
	of the highlights.

	June 23rd, /oracle817 being full.  Odds are, the file
	system was full.  Migrate it to a larger device, remove
	unused files or find a way to give it more space.

	July 6th, AdvFS I/O error.  Probably a real I/O error
	on the underlying device.  Could merely be a bad block
	or two, but it could be problem particular to the
	device; rz13.

	July 8th, System and Process corrected errors.  These
	are probably CPU and system problems.

	July 10th, Adapter level I/O errors.  Could be the
	device out at the end of the particular I/O adapter,
	or it could be the adapter itself.  The I/O error on
	rz11 could be related, since it is on the same bus
	as the reported bus/target/LUN, but it is a different
	device (bus 1, target 5, LUN 6 would rzg13, in V4
	naming convention).

	Find the error log translation/analysis tool that works
	best for your system and take a close look at the events
	that were logged around the time of the event listed
	in the messages file.  If the system is on a service
	contract or under warranty, contact the appropriate
	service vendor.  The usual choices are uerf(8), DECevent
	and Compaq Analyze.  Which works for your system, depends
	on the model of system and I didn't see that listed.

	If suspect a close association of the problems, look at
	the environment where the system is sitting.  Make sure
	the temperature and humidity are within the range allowed
	for the system.  Lots of problems can be heat induced if
	it is summer where you are.

	Alan Rollow

